{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When a person has on the skin of his flesh a swelling, a rash, or a bright spot.</b> We learned in the Mishnah: there are four appearances of negaim (skin-afflictions): שאת (se'et, 'swelling') and its derivative, בהרת (baheret, 'bright spot') and its derivative. בהרת is intense white like snow; its derivative is like the lime of the Sanctuary. שאת is white like wool; its derivative is like the membrane of an egg. The hue of all four is white, and this whiteness is one rank above the other. ספחת (sapachat, 'rash') is the name of the affliction that is joined from both of them — from שאת and from בהרת — from the language of (I Samuel 2:36) ״ספחני נא אל אחת הכהנות״ ('attach me, please, to one of the priestly families'), and (ibid. 26) ״מהסתפח בנחלת ה׳״ ('that I may be attached to the inheritance of HaShem'). These appearances of afflictions are not צרעת (tzara'at), but it is possible that they may come to צרעת. Because of this, the Torah set this illness apart at its very beginning — and this is what the medical scholars said: בהרות (bright spots) — from them צרעת can be discerned. And this is what the verse says: נגע צרעת, that is, a stroke of צרעת; it is not absolute צרעת. But after the quarantine, when complete signs of impurity are found, so that the verse says צרעת היא — this is absolute צרעת. Therefore you will find that sometimes it says וטמאו הכהן נגע צרעת היא, or it says נגע צרעת היא וראהו הכהן וטמא אותו — the intention is to say that he declares him impure from now, for it is a nega (affliction) that will certainly come to צרעת, and it is fitting that he be separated from the people from this time onward. And so when it says וטמא אותו הכהן נגע הוא — its meaning is that it is a great nega from which he will never heal, but it will grow all day and spread. Thus explained the Ramban, z\"l. And behold: the spreading, and white hair, and healthy flesh, and depth of the skin — all are signs of impurity." "<b>And in the Midrash.</b> 'When a person has on the skin of his flesh' — why does it not say in this parashah דבר אל בני ישראל ('speak to the children of Israel') as is written in the other parashiot? Because it says (Psalms 5:5) ״כי לא אל חפץ רשע אתה לא יגורך רע״ — to teach you that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not wish to incriminate any creature, as it says (Ezekiel 33:11) ״חי אני נאם ה׳ אלהים אם אחפוץ במות הרשע״. And what does He wish? To vindicate His creatures, as it says (Isaiah 42:21–22) ״ה׳ חפץ למען צדקו יגדיל תורה ויאדיר״ — He wishes to vindicate His creatures and not to incriminate them; therefore He did not say in the parashah of negaim דבר אל בני ישראל. Another thing: ״לא יגורך רע״ — the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He, is not mentioned upon evil but only upon good. Know that this is so: when the Holy One, blessed be He, created light and darkness and called them names, He mentioned His Name upon the light and did not mention His Name upon the darkness, as it says (Genesis 1:5) ״ויקרא אלהים לאור יום ולחשך קרא לילה״. And likewise you find that when He blessed the human and Chavah He mentioned His Name upon them, as it says (ibid.) ״ויברך אותם אלהים״, but when He cursed them He did not mention His Name upon them, but rather (ibid. 3) ״אל האשה אמר״, ״ולאדם אמר״. And if you would say regarding the serpent that He mentioned His Name in its curse, as it says (ibid.) ״ויאמר ה׳ אלהים אל הנחש כי עשית זאת״ — thus our Sages, of blessed memory, taught: for three things He mentioned His Name even though they are for evil: regarding the inciter — this is the serpent, who incited the woman and said to her ״כי יודע אלהים כי ביום אכלכם ממנו ונפקחו עיניכם והייתם כאלהים״ — 'you will be like Him': just as He creates worlds, so you too will create worlds, for every craftsman hates the sons of his craft; and because he incited and spoke this evil tongue, He mentioned His Name upon him. And regarding one who transgresses the words of the Sages, who is liable to death, as it says (Jeremiah 11:3) ״כה אמר ה׳ אלהי ישראל ארור האיש אשר לא ישמע את דברי הברית הזאת״. And regarding one who places his trust in flesh and blood, as it says (ibid. 16) ״כה אמר ה׳ ארור הגבר אשר יבטח באדם ושם בשר זרועו ומן ה׳ יסור לבו״ — when is he cursed? When his heart turns away from HaShem. And so you find with Noah: when he blessed Shem he mentioned HaShem and said (Genesis 9:26) ״ברוך ה׳ אלהי שם״, and when he cursed Canaan he did not mention Him, as it says ״ארור כנען״. And so with Elisha the prophet: he mentioned HaShem in his prayer regarding deliverance, as it says (II Kings 6:17) ״ה׳ פקח נא את עיניו ויראה״, but when he cursed the Arameans he said (ibid.) ״הך נא את הגוי הזה בסנורים״ and did not mention HaShem in the punishment; and when he turned back and prayed for them regarding deliverance, he mentioned HaShem and said (ibid.) ״פקח את עיני אלה ויראו״. From this you may say that the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He, is not mentioned upon evil. And even with Israel, to whom He gave honor, He did not mention them upon evil — how so? When He came to give warning about the offerings, what does He say? ״דבר אל בני ישראל וגו׳ אדם כי יקריב מכם קרבן לה׳״. But when He came to give warning about the negaim, He mentioned only ״אדם כי יהיה בעור בשרו״. For what reason do the negaim come? Upon illicit relations. And so you find in Jerusalem: because they were steeped in lust, therefore they were stricken with צרעת. What is written there? (Isaiah 3:16) ״ויאמר ה׳ יען כי גבהו בנות ציון ותלכנה נטויות גרון״. What did the Holy One, blessed be He, do to them? He struck them with צרעת, as it says (ibid.) ״ושפח ה׳ קדקד בנות ציון״, and שפח is nothing but צרעת, as it says ״ולשאת ולספחת ולבהרת״. 'When a person has on the skin of his flesh a שאת' — this is what the verse says (Habakkuk 1:7) ״איום ונורא הוא ממנו משפטו ושאתו יצא״. This verse speaks of the first man, whom the Holy One, blessed be He, created in His likeness, as it says (Genesis 1:27) ״ויברא אלהים את האדם בצלמו״, and when He created him, He created him from one end of the world to the other, as it says (Deuteronomy 4:32) ״כי שאל נא לימים ראשונים״, and he had dominion over the whole world, as it says (Genesis 1:28) ״ורדו בדגת הים ובעוף השמים״. ״ממנו משפטו ושאתו יצא״ — this is Chavah, who came forth from him and caused him death, as it says (ibid. 3) ״ותתן גם לאישה עמה ויאכל״. Another interpretation: ״איום ונורא הוא״ — this is the wicked Pharaoh, who ruled over the whole world, as it says (Psalms 105:20) ״מושל עמים ויפתחהו״. ״ממנו משפטו ושאתו יצא״ — this is Moshe, who was raised in his house, and he supposed he was a member of his household, as it says (Exodus 2:10) ״ויגדל הילד ותביאהו לבת פרעה״, and he arose and brought upon him ten plagues, as it says (ibid. 3) ״ועתה לכה ואשלחך אל פרעה״. Said R. Yehudah bar Ami: the staff bore a load of forty se'ah and was of sanpirinon, and the ten plagues were inscribed upon it in notarikon: דצ״ך עד״ש באח״ב, and Moshe would look at the staff and see which plague was fit to come upon Pharaoh, and bring it upon him — hence ״איום ונורא הוא״. Another interpretation: ״איום ונורא הוא״ — this is Edom, as it says (Daniel 7:7) ״וארו חויא רביעאה דחילא ואמתני ותקיפא יתירה ושנין די פרזל לה״. ״ממנו משפטו ושאתו יצא״ — this is the prophet Ovadiah, who was an Edomite convert, and he prophesied against it its destruction. 'When a person has on the skin of his flesh שאת or ספחת or בהרת and it is on the skin of his flesh for nega צרעת' — so our Sages, of blessed memory: שאת — this is Babylon, as it says (Isaiah 14:4) ״ונשאת המשל הזה על מלך בבל״. ספחת — this is the kingdom of Media, for Haman attached himself (מסתפח) with Achashverosh to destroy, slay, and annihilate. בהרת — this is Greece, which was 'making bright' (מבהרת) against Israel and would say: whoever has an ox, let him engrave on the horn of the ox — 'I have no portion in the Elohim of Israel'; and if not, they decree upon him execution. ״והיה בעור בשרו לנגע צרעת״ — this is the kingdom of Aram, which the Holy One, blessed be He, will strike with צרעת along with its prince; for thus prophesied Yirmeyahu, peace be upon him (Jeremiah 46:15) ״מדוע נסחף אביריך לא עמד כי ה׳ הדפו״ — and the word נסחף is like נספח from the language of ספחת; and it says אביריך with a yod, because the prince thereof includes all the אבירים (mighty ones) under his hand — the אביר (Mighty One) of Yaakov will come and exact retribution from that אביר. And you should be roused to note that we do not find the word אביר regarding the Holy One, blessed be He, with a dagesh, but only with rafe — and the meaning is the attribute of mercy; and this is (Genesis 49:24) ״מידי אביר יעקב״, (Psalms 132:2) ״נדר לאביר יעקב״, and in the prayer of Ana: אביר יעקב — 'the Strength of Yaakov'; but regarding the might of the kingdom of Aram it says אביריך with a dagesh. And also regarding Doeg the Edomite you find אביר with a dagesh — thus it is written (I Samuel 21:8) ״ושמו דואג האדומי אביר הרועים אשר לשאול״, and even though he was an Israelite, a member of the Sanhedrin, our Sages, of blessed memory, already expounded that he was so called after his city, which is Edom. And the Midrash concludes and says: and if Na'aman, through the merit that he took captive a young girl, was stricken with צרעת — the kingdom of Aram, which enslaved Israel, all the more so! And from where do we know that the Holy One, blessed be He, exacts retribution from it and from its prince with צרעת? For thus prophesied Zecharyah (Zechariah 14:12) ״וזאת תהיה המגפה אשר יגוף ה׳ את כל העמים אשר צבאו על ירושלים המק בשרו והוא עומד על רגליו וגו׳״ — end of the Midrash." "<b>And adjacent.</b> This parashah of negaim is adjacent to the parashah of the woman who gives birth, to teach you that anyone who is not careful regarding the menstruant (niddah) — the child will be a מצורע (metzora). And it is found in the books of the wisdom of nature, and so our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: whoever lies with a woman on the first day of her menstruation — the child will be a מצורע for ten years; and if he lies with her for two days of her menstruation — he will be a מצורע for twenty years; and if for three days of her menstruation — he will be a מצורע for thirty years; and so on up to seven days; and if on the seventh day — he will be a מצורע for seventy years. From here it follows that in every adjacency of the parashiot in the Torah one to another there is a great principle and wondrous wisdom — both in the wisdom of nature and in the other wisdoms, for the Torah is inclusive of them all."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {1 "Rabbeinu Bahya cites the first three words of Habakkuk 1:7 from its second stich (ממנו משפטו ושאתו יצא), which the Midrash reads as 'from him his judgment and his שאת went forth'; the word שאת is read as a noun meaning 'elevation/pride' in its biblical context but is here linked homiletically to the skin-affliction שאת of our verse. The citation from Psalms 105:20 is given as מושל עמים ויפתחהו; the standard verse numbering may vary. The notarikon דצ״ך עד״ש באח״ב is the traditional acronym for the ten plagues.", 2 "The lemma ונסמכה ('and adjacent') refers to the rabbinic principle of סמיכות הפרשיות (adjacency of scriptural sections); the comment opens mid-thought with this participle as its heading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:52.841381003Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}